What Is Sampling in Application Insights / Observability?

Sampling means:
Collecting only a percentage of telemetry instead of collecting EVERYTHING.

Used to reduce:
storage cost
ingestion cost
network overhead
performance impact

WHY SAMPLING NEEDED?
In large AKS/microservice environments:

Imagine:
100 microservices
10k requests/sec

If every:
request
trace
dependency
log

is stored:
Telemetry volume becomes HUGE.

Example Without Sampling
1 million requests/hour

If every trace stored:
massive storage
high Azure Monitor ingestion cost
slow queries
WITH SAMPLING

Collect only:

10%

Example:

1,000,000 requests
↓
100,000 stored

Still enough for:

troubleshooting
trends
anomaly detection

BUT much cheaper.

VERY IMPORTANT UNDERSTANDING

Sampling DOES NOT mean:

randomly losing all visibility

Modern sampling tries to preserve:

distributed traces
important failures
representative telemetry
TYPES OF SAMPLING
1. FIXED-RATE SAMPLING

Simple percentage.

Example:

Collect 10% telemetry
Example

1000 requests:

store only 100
2. ADAPTIVE SAMPLING (Very Common)

Automatically adjusts sampling rate based on traffic volume.

Example:

Low traffic:

collect 100%

High traffic:

collect 5%

Very useful for enterprise systems.

3. TRACE-BASED SAMPLING

Important in distributed tracing.

Ensures:

entire request trace preserved

instead of partial spans.

Example

Request flow:

Frontend → Auth → Payment → DB

Sampling ensures:

whole transaction kept
OR
whole transaction dropped

Not partial.

Very important.

HOW SAMPLING REDUCES COST

Azure Monitor/App Insights pricing largely based on:

data ingestion volume

More telemetry:
→ more GB/day
→ more cost

Example

Without sampling:

500 GB/day

With 10% sampling:

50 GB/day

Huge savings.

WHERE SAMPLING CONFIGURED?

Depends on architecture.

OPTION 1 — APPLICATION INSIGHTS SDK

Very common.

